Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two of the fourteen Gewerke had their own top-level tab and neither had
anything on it. /bvb-eea and /psv-itk were eight-column read-only tables:
no KPIs, no search, no filters, no card view, no map, and no way to open a
project. The Projekte page had all of it. Two pages showing the same 1,298
projects through a different filter should not be two different products.

ReviewWorkspace is one implementation of that surface, scoped to one
Gewerk, and the two routes are now ten-line wrappers around it — the same
arrangement DepartmentReviewTable introduced, extended from the table to
the whole page. DepartmentReviewTable is deleted rather than left as a
second thing to keep true.

Scoping rule, applied once and reused by every count, chip, marker and
export on the page: a project appears when its review row for this
department normalises to something other than "nicht erforderlich". The
normalisation is what makes it correct — ITK stores 3 rows as
"Projektkonfiguration" against 51 as "Projektkonfig.", and matching the raw
string drops them. Because the KPI row, the result count, the table, the
cards, the map and the CSV all derive from that one set, they cannot
disagree; the smoke suite now asserts exactly that, against a count it
derives independently from data.json.

The Änderungshistorie recorded field edits and nothing else. A user could
export four PDFs, hand three prefilled mails to Outlook and save a draft,
and the log would say they did nothing all afternoon.

- shared/audit-actions.ts closes the vocabulary. Every action now has a
  tone in a total Record<AuditAction, AuditTone>, so adding an action
  without giving it a tone stops the build. AuditLog.tsx used a chain of
  regexes over the action text, so "PDF erzeugt" fell through to an
  anonymous blue row.
- client.ts wrote four free-text strings, one of which ("Projekt erstellt")
  no longer matched anything else in the app. All four go through the
  vocabulary; the old phrases stay resolvable, because a user's local trail
  is append-only and there is no migration step.
- Documents, exports and prefilled messages record. "vorbereitet", not
  "gesendet": a mailto: hands the message to the user's client and the app
  never learns what happened next. A test asserts no action string can ever
  say gesendet, zugestellt or gedruckt.

Two data-integrity defects the wizard had, both found by reading the
booking path:

- submit() wrote "· Termin 28.05. 09:00" into the audit trail *before*
  bookSlot() ran. A slot taken by someone else in the meantime sent the
  user back to step 4 with a booking in the log that never happened. The
  entry now says "Wunschtermin"; the booking gets its own entry from the
  caller, after bookSlot has actually succeeded.
- That retry then called draft.submit() again, creating a *second* project
  with the same Projektnummer. One rejected booking was enough to duplicate
  a project. The project is created once and held; a retry only books. And
  if the replacement slot falls on a different day, terminProjektvorstellung
  is updated through the normal mutation, so the project and the booking
  cannot end up on two different dates.

Gates: tsc clean, biome unchanged at 72 pre-existing warnings, 174 unit
tests, build clean, e2e 49/49 (12 of them new), check-ui clean across
6 routes x 6 scenarios x 3 viewports x 4 theme combinations.
