From 4465a51146ec4348dfcadb604e42840cf297de6f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 18:16:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] one search engine for the whole site, one status control
 for every Gewerk cell, and a stress gate that proves the three tabs are
 independent
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

SEARCH
The header box searched almost nothing. searchSuggestions walked all 1,298
projects on every keystroke, matched raw substrings on untouched strings
and returned whichever ten came first in file order. "Muenchen" found
nothing for "München". A typo found nothing. It could not find a Gewerk, a
page, a view, a status or a region at all, and every suggestion — whatever
it was — did the same thing: dump the text into the Projekte filter.

shared/search.ts is the whole engine: a pre-folded, pre-tokenised index and
a scorer ranking exact over prefix over word-prefix over substring over
fuzzy, with an entry's own name worth 150 more than something it merely
mentions. German folding is done twice, expanded (ä→ae) and stripped
(ä→a), on both index and query, so "München", "Muenchen" and "Munchen"
land on the same station.

Two performance defects were found by their own tests rather than in
production. The first scorer ran an edit distance against every word of
every entry that failed to match: 25 ms per keystroke, slower than the
typing it was meant to follow. Corrections are now a second pass over
labels only, and only when nothing strong turned up — 3 ms. The second was
building the index at mount: a few hundred milliseconds of main thread on
every navigation, for a box most visits never touch. useSearchIndex builds
on first use and shares one copy through a WeakMap keyed on the projects
array.

shared/search-index.ts makes everything nameable findable — pages, views,
Gewerke, stations, people, regions, statuses, and every identifier a
project carries including its Strecken-Nr. Each entry knows where it goes:
a Gewerk opens its own tab, "Karte" opens the map, a station opens its
projects as cards. CommandSearch is a real WAI-ARIA combobox — grouped,
keyboard-first, Ctrl/⌘-K from anywhere, "Meinten Sie …?" offered and never
silently applied. The page filter boxes are the same index through
FilterSearch, minus pages and views, which would navigate away from the
filter being built.

Its accessible name is deliberately "Website durchsuchen — Projekte, Orte,
Personen und Seiten". The old one contained "Stationen", so a by-name
lookup for the wizard's *station field* resolved to the global search and
its result list covered the step buttons underneath. A control present on
every route must not answer to every field name in the app.

STATUS
Every Gewerk status is now changeable everywhere: the two Gewerk tabs and
all 14 columns on Projekte, collapsed or expanded. Each change goes through
the same audited, optimistic, rolled-back mutation as any other edit.

The obvious implementation was measured and rejected. 1,298 rows x 14
Gewerke is 18,172 selects carrying ~236,000 option nodes; the table took
10.0 s to paint against 4.6 s before, and the smoke suite found it empty
where it had found 1,298 rows. The control is the click-to-edit shape
InlineEditCell already uses: a button that reads as the badge, and a real
select the moment it is activated. Element count is now within one of the
old read-only badges. A first attempt wrapping the text in a span with a
lucide chevron added 54,000 nodes on its own; the affordance is a CSS
::after instead.

The vocabulary always contains the value already stored, even when that is
outside REVIEW_STATUSES. A select that cannot represent its own value
renders blank and the next change rewrites a status nobody meant to touch.

TABLE
Nr. and Projektnummer pin to the left of both tables. Scrolling twelve — or
twenty-seven — columns sideways used to take the row's identity off screen
and leave a wall of dates and statuses belonging to nothing a reader could
name. InlineEditCell lost its -mx-1: the button measured four pixels wider
than its own cell, which was invisible until the identity columns were
given a fixed width and then reported at every viewport.

GATES
Two blind spots closed, both of which had been hiding real defects:

  - every scenario measured tables at scrollLeft 0, so the columns past the
    fold were never audited — which is exactly where the old badge-and-
    select pair sat on top of each other with the row actions over them.
    Two scrolled-table scenarios and a search-results scenario now run.
  - the overlap check treated a sticky column and a floating listbox as
    text-on-text. Both are now recognised as legitimate cover — narrowly:
    position:absolute is still audited, because that is what the map's old
    legend-covering card was. Its opacity test also learned oklch(), which
    Tailwind v4 emits: dark:bg-zinc-950 was read as transparent, so dark
    mode reported 23 phantom overlaps and light mode none.

scripts/stress-test.mjs is new and answers what a single-pass smoke test
cannot: twenty tab switches leak no search, view or filter; each tab scopes
to its own Gewerk on every visit; each tab creates and destroys its own
map, and the three draw different markers; forty route changes leave no
Leaflet container behind; typing a station never blocks a frame and 200
searches do not degrade; thirty rapid status changes all persist a reload
and all reach the Änderungshistorie.

Gates: tsc clean, biome unchanged at 72 pre-existing warnings, 198 unit
tests, build clean, e2e 63/63 (11 new), stress 8/8, check-ui clean across
6 routes x 9 scenarios x 3 viewports x 4 theme combinations.
